     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet because the dose looks unsafe and I want to check it with you first.\n\n**The problem:** 1700 mg three times daily adds up to **5100 mg per day**. That's double the maximum labeled dose of metformin:\n- **Immediate-release:** maximum 2550 mg/day (usually 850 mg three times daily)\n- **Extended-release:** maximum 2000 mg/day\n\nA dose this high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and serious stomach and bowel side effects, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**Did you mean one of these?**\n1. **Metformin 850 mg by mouth, three times daily with meals.** That's 2550 mg/day, the maximum immediate-release dose. If \"1700\" came from a per-day figure, this is a likely match.\n2. **Metformin 1700 mg per day total, split into doses.** For example, 850 mg twice daily with meals.\n3. **Something else.** For example, the extended-release form, or a lower starting dose if the patient is new to metformin. People usually start at 500 mg once or twice daily and increase slowly.\n\nPlease confirm the dose and formulation you want, and I'll enter the order right away."
      }
     ],
